<BaseLayout
  title="Music — Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits? | Nazaryah"
  description="Yahuah gave His people a song. Music from the heart to Yahuah is worship. Music from a stage into the heart is something else."
>
  <article class="cod-article">
    <div class="cod-article-inner">
      <a href="/" class="cod-back"><span class="arrow">←</span><span>Back to Home</span></a>

      <header class="cod-title-block">
        <p class="cod-chapter-label">Chapter Two · Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits?</p>
        <h1 class="cod-title">Music</h1>
        <p class="cod-subtitle">The Sound of Self-Worship</p>
        <p class="cod-tagline">Yahuah gave His people a song. Music from the heart to Yahuah is worship. Music from a stage into the heart is something else.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="cod-pullquote">Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits? Let Scripture and history decide.</p>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">INTRODUCTION</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Question No One Is Asking</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">A believer today can wake up to earbuds, drive to work with the Christian station on, open a worship playlist at lunch, stream a concert after dinner, and put the children to sleep with a bedtime playlist on a smart speaker. By nightfall he has taken in four, five, or six hours of music — none of which came out of him, and all of which went into him.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Singing is commanded. Praise is commanded. A heart lifted to Yahuah is commanded. The question is different: where in Scripture is the believer told to sit and let a stranger's music flow into him for hours a day, or that someone else's song is a doorway to the Father? It is not there.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture names the power behind a practice that turns believers from the truth: "some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits" (1 Timothy 4:1). Against it stands the Spirit of truth, who "will guide you into all truth" (John 16:13). <strong>In music, the enemy took the worship.</strong> The song stayed, and the room is full of it, but it was turned from Yahuah to the singer and how he feels. "This people draweth nigh unto me with their mouth … but their heart is far from me" (Matthew 15:8).</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">I</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Oldest Tool</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Music belongs to Yahuah. Miriam sang it at the Red Sea, David played it before the ark, and the Levites were set apart to lead it in His house (1 Chronicles 25:1). But Scripture also shows, from its first pages, that music was one of the first gifts turned away from Him. "And Cain went out from the presence of Yahuah … And his brother's name was Jubal: he was the father of all such as handle the harp and organ" (Genesis 4:16, 21).</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The first musician named in Scripture comes from the line of Cain, the man who "went out from the presence of" Yahuah and built the first city (Genesis 4:16-17). The next voice in that line is Lamech, whose words are the first song or poem recorded in the Bible, and it is a boast of murder: "I have slain a man to my wounding" (Genesis 4:23). The harp is not evil; David played one. But the first time music appears, it is in the family that walked away from Yahuah's presence. Two Hebrew words name the instruments: <em>kinnor</em>, the harp or lyre — strings and melody — and <em>ugab</em>, the pipe — wind, breath, and the sustained note.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">From that line forward, Scripture keeps showing the same thing. At Sinai, Moses heard the singing before he saw the calf (Exodus 32:18-19). On the plain of Dura, the music was the signal to bow before the golden image (Daniel 3:5). Isaiah watched the nobles of Judah feast with "the harp, and the viol, the tabret, and pipe," while "they regard not the work of" Yahuah (Isaiah 5:12). When Babylon falls, her "harpers, and musicians, and of pipers, and trumpeters" go silent with her (Revelation 18:22).</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The adversary does not need a song of his own. He is "transformed into an angel of light," and his ministers "as the ministers of righteousness" (2 Corinthians 11:14-15). He takes what Yahuah made good and turns it the other way. Nearly every religion on earth puts music at the center of its worship — temple chant, monastery bells, ritual drumming, the whirling of the Sufis. <strong>Music is not neutral, because it moves people.</strong> It moved a nation to dance around a calf and an empire to its knees before an image. That is why a seducing spirit reaches for it first.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">II</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Direction Decides Everything</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Music in Scripture is never judged by style, instrument, or volume. It is judged by direction. Scripture authorizes two directions, and the church has added a third.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Upward: Worship</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Music that flows out of the worshiper, up to Yahuah. Miriam sang after the Red Sea: "Sing ye to Yahuah" (Exodus 15:21). David wrote psalms from his heart to the Father. Paul told the Ephesians to make melody "in your heart to the Master" (Ephesians 5:19). Every accepted instance of music in Scripture moves this way — out of a heart already changed, rising as praise.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Across: Edification</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Music that flows between believers as equals. Paul told the Colossians to teach and admonish "one another in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs" (Colossians 3:16). Every believer sings, every believer teaches, every believer is admonished. There is no stage, no performer, and no audience. This is the music of the Spirit of truth: up to Yahuah, and across to one another.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Inward: The Direction Scripture Never Endorses</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Music that flows one way, from a performer on a platform into a listening crowd. One gives; many receive. The receivers are shaped, moved, and stirred by what comes into them. This is the model of the modern Christian music industry, the Sunday morning band, Christian radio, the curated playlist, and the livestream concert. When one voice is amplified over all the others and the room depends on a performer to keep the music going, the singing has left mutual edification and become performance. <strong>Outflow is worship. Inflow is manipulation.</strong> And inflow is the direction a seducing spirit always prefers, because it reaches the heart without passing through the will.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — When Music Is Used on People</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">Scripture shows the one-way inflow model every time it records music being used on a person.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Saul's spirit.</strong> Saul had an evil spirit, and David's music affected it (1 Samuel 16:23). The music acted on the spirit. The text says the evil spirit departed; it never says the Spirit of Yahuah arrived. Spirits respond to music. That is a warning, not a worship manual.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Sinai and the golden calf.</strong> Moses and Joshua came down the mountain and heard singing before they saw the calf (Exodus 32:18). The music came before the idolatry and prepared the people for it. The Hebrew word for what they "rose up to" do (Exodus 32:6) is <em>tsachaq</em>, the same word used of Isaac sporting with Rebekah (Genesis 26:8), a word with sexual undertones. The festival turned carnal. Music stirs the flesh, and the flesh turns fast.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The plain of Dura.</strong> Nebuchadnezzar set up a golden image and told the crowd: "at what time ye hear the sound of the cornet, flute, harp, sackbut, psaltery, dulcimer, and all kinds of musick, ye fall down and worship the golden image" (Daniel 3:5). Six instruments and every kind of music — a full orchestra — and it was the trigger to bow. The music did not express the worship. It caused it.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Three who stood.</strong> Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego stood straight when the music played, because they knew the Law, and the music did not change the Law (Daniel 3:12). When a crowd goes down because the chord progression told it to — when hands go up and tears fall not because the Word was read but because the build-up hit the right note — the believer's posture is theirs. Do not bow just because you were cued to.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">III</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Song Changes Hands (A.D. 100-1900)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The first assemblies sang psalms, hymns, and spiritual songs together (Colossians 3:16; James 5:13), and Paul and Silas sang in prison at midnight (Acts 16:25). For centuries the church's singing was mostly unaccompanied. Several early church writers spoke against instruments in worship because they belonged to the pagan theater and the feasts of the idols.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The organ came in slowly. In 757 the Byzantine emperor sent an organ as a gift to the Frankish king Pepin, and by the late 900s England's Winchester Cathedral had a great organ with hundreds of pipes. Over the Middle Ages the singing moved from the congregation to trained choirs of clergy and monks, in Latin the people did not speak. The people listened.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The Reformation pulled in two directions. Luther wrote hymns in German for the whole congregation to sing. In Zurich, Zwingli had the organ in the Great Minster taken out in 1527. Calvin's Geneva sang only the Psalms, set to plain tunes, without instruments, and completed its Psalter in 1562. Then in 1707 Isaac Watts published <em>Hymns and Spiritual Songs</em>, and English-speaking churches began singing songs of human composition alongside the Psalms. The Wesleys wrote thousands more.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">In the 1870s the evangelist Dwight L. Moody brought along a singer, Ira Sankey, who sang solos before the sermon and during the invitation. Their songbooks sold in the millions. A song was now part of the method, set in the service to move the crowd toward a decision. Chapter Six traced where that went: the invitation hymn sung "one more verse" until the aisle filled. <strong>The song moved from the congregation's mouth to the singer's stage, and from praise to Yahuah to a tool for moving people.</strong> That is the shift a seducing spirit needed.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">IV</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Stage (1960s-Today)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">In the late 1960s the Jesus Movement brought young people from the California counterculture into the churches, and they brought their music. In 1971 Calvary Chapel in Costa Mesa started Maranatha! Music to record its bands. Christian rock and pop grew into an industry with its own labels, radio stations, charts, and awards; the Dove Awards began in 1969. By the 1990s "praise and worship" albums were among the best-selling Christian recordings, and the "worship leader" with a band had replaced the hymnbook in many churches.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Every song sung in those churches is now tracked. Since 1988 Christian Copyright Licensing International has licensed churches to project and copy songs, collected reports of what each church sings, and paid royalties to the songwriters and publishers. The songs sung most on Sunday are the songs that pay most on Monday. A handful of large churches — Hillsong, Bethel, Elevation — write and publish much of what the rest of the world sings.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The prophet Ezekiel was told what such a hearer becomes: "lo, thou art unto them as a very lovely song of one that hath a pleasant voice, and can play well on an instrument: for they hear thy words, but they do them not" (Ezekiel 33:32). <strong>A song enjoyed and never obeyed is the seducing spirit's favorite kind of worship.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — The Worship Business</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The license.</strong> Christian Copyright Licensing International was founded in 1988 by Howard Rachinski, a worship pastor in Oregon who saw that churches were copying songs onto overhead transparencies and into bulletins without permission. CCLI sold churches a yearly license, priced by size, that allowed them to project and print songs from its catalog. Churches report the songs they use, and CCLI divides the license fees among the songwriters and publishers by usage. Its "Top 100" list shows which songs are sung most in licensed churches.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The labels.</strong> Integrity Music was founded in 1987 and built its business on "praise and worship" recordings, sold first by mail and later in stores. Hillsong Church in Sydney, begun in 1983, released the song <em>Shout to the Lord</em> in 1994, and it spread around the world. Hillsong's music arm grew into a global label with touring bands. Bethel Music was formed in 2001 in Redding, California, and Elevation Worship in 2007 in Charlotte, North Carolina. Each releases albums, licenses songs, sells merchandise, and tours.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The money.</strong> A song that enters the CCLI top lists can earn its writers and publishers steady income for years, paid every time a church reports singing it. Streaming adds more. Large worship ministries earn from licensing, recordings, conferences, and schools. The church that sings a song every week is paying, through its license, the ministry that wrote it.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The sound.</strong> The modern worship song is built like popular music: a verse, a chorus, a bridge, and a long build to a loud climax, often repeated several times. Bands use in-ear monitors, click tracks, lighting cues, and haze. The arrangement is designed to carry the room to an emotional peak, and the peak is often called the moment Yahuah showed up.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Paul's word for men who sold the Word was <em>kapēleuō</em>, to peddle it like a merchant: "we are not as many, which corrupt the word of God" (2 Corinthians 2:17).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">V</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Piper Is Still Calling</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">One of the best-known rock songs of the 1970s, by Led Zeppelin, sings of a piper calling the listener to come and join him. The band always denied that the song was about anything dark, but the picture is old. It goes back to the Pied Piper of legend, the musician whose tune was so irresistible that the children followed him out of town and were never seen again.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Modern Christianity has built an industry on the idea that the piper has been converted: change the lyrics, add the Name to the chorus, and plug the same rhythms, the same production, and the same audio tricks into the same human body. But the body does not read the lyrics before the beat hits. <strong>Music enters the body before the words are understood. The beat does its work first.</strong> By the time the mind weighs the words, the flesh has already been moved. That order — the feeling first, the truth later or never — is a seducing spirit's order.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — The Daily Dose</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">The worship band on Sunday morning is not the largest part of the problem. It has the believer for forty minutes a week. The real exposure is the rest of the week: Christian radio on the commute, the worship playlist at the gym, the streaming service that runs while dinner is cooked, the bedtime app for the children. Six hours a day, seven days a week, music flows into the believer from strangers he has never met, whose doctrine he has never examined, and whose lives he does not know.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">No believer would let a stranger preach to his children six hours a day, or sit under a pastor whose teaching he had never checked. But he will let a musician do both, through every speaker in the house, every day. Because it is called worship, the filter never goes up.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">A song does everything a sermon does and more. It teaches doctrine, shapes emotion, and trains the heart to tie certain feelings to certain phrases. It carves grooves into the mind that are harder to erase than any sermon, because they are carried on melody, and melody sticks. If the doctrine in the song is wrong, the hearer absorbs wrong doctrine at the speed of song. If the lyrics make the Father a lover, soften the Son, or erase the Law, it is poured in through a door that goes around his defenses and straight to his feelings.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The piper is still calling. He is calling through a subscription service now.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VI</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Naming the Pipers</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The following movements produce much of the music on the radio, in the apps, and on the screens of modern churches.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Hillsong</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">The global brand behind some of the most sung worship songs on earth. The founder's father, Frank Houston, sexually abused children; Brian Houston was charged with concealing it and acquitted in 2023. Its teaching leans toward the prosperity gospel, and many of its lyrics use romantic language that could be sung to a human lover.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Bethel (Redding)</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Under Bill Johnson, Bethel teaches that Yahushua laid aside His divine power and did His miracles as a man empowered by the Spirit, so that any believer can do the same. The ministry is known for reports of gold dust and feathers, "grave soaking" at the graves of past revivalists, and fire tunnels. A church that sings Bethel songs every week for a year sings Bethel's teaching. The songs are the delivery system.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Elevation Church</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Steven Furtick has preached that "God broke the law for love" — but Yahuah does not break His own Law. In 2014 a Charlotte television station reported that the church had planted volunteers to come forward at its "spontaneous" baptism events, on cue, to get the crowd moving. Elevation Worship carries the same methods into music.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Jesus Culture</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">The youth-revival offshoot of Bethel: the same teaching and the same sound, aimed at teenagers and young adults, shaping a generation's picture of what a Messiah looks like.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Passion and Chris Tomlin</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Louie Giglio's Passion conferences have gathered tens of thousands of college students each year since 1997, and Chris Tomlin, who came up through Passion, has been one of the most-sung worship songwriters in American churches.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">IHOP — International House of Prayer</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Mike Bickle built a ministry on worship and prayer around the clock in Kansas City and was accused of decades of sexual misconduct; the ministry cut ties with him in December 2023. Its teaching framed Yahushua as a lover and the believer as His intimate bride, and the music that came out of it carries that picture wherever it travels.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">The New Apostolic Reformation</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">The umbrella over many of these movements. It teaches a restored office of modern apostles and prophets with authority over churches and nations (Chapter Nine traces where that came from). Its songs are full of declaring, decreeing, and "shifting the atmosphere."</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">Hebrew Roots and Messianic Music</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Coming out of Babylon does not mean carrying her music methods along. Messianic concerts and Hebrew-language worship albums with the same production, the same stage, and the same one-way flow are still inflow. Hebrew words sprinkled on top do not change the direction.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">One Mark Across All of Them</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">They all set aside the Law. They ignore the Sabbath Yahuah set, skip the feasts of Leviticus 23, and preach a Messiah who came to do away with what Yahushua said He did not come to do away with (Matthew 5:17-19). Where the Law is missing, the music is untethered. The doctrine in the lyrics cannot rise higher than the doctrine in the ministry. The Spirit of truth leads into His commandments (John 14:15-17); a song that leads away from them is carried by another spirit.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Amos Already Said It</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <blockquote class="cod-scripture">
            <p class="cod-scripture-text">I hate, I despise your feast days … Take thou away from me the noise of thy songs; for I will not hear the melody of thy viols</p>
            <p class="cod-scripture-ref">— Amos 5:21, 23</p>
          </blockquote>
          <p class="cod-body">A nation offering sincere, skillful worship — feast days, offerings, and songs — and Yahuah calls it noise and tells them to take it away. The people had abandoned His Law and turned "aside the poor in the gate" (Amos 5:12). Music that rises from obedience is a sweet offering. Music that rises from comfortable unrighteousness is noise He wants silenced. The style does not change that, the production does not change it, and the sincerity of the worshiper does not change it. The Law decides.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">"That chant to the sound of the viol, and invent to themselves instruments of musick, like David … but they are not grieved for the affliction of Joseph" (Amos 6:5-6). This is a woe on people who make music to be like David while ignoring the suffering of their brothers.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The modern worship industry has arena tours, merchandise lines, and ticket prices for the evening where the audience is told it will meet with God. Amos's word for that is woe.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Scripture Shows Instead</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">This is the song of the Spirit of truth.</p>

      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>Sing to Yahuah, from the heart.</strong> "Singing and making melody in your heart to the Master" (Ephesians 5:19).</li>
        <li><strong>Sing to one another, as equals.</strong> "Teaching and admonishing one another in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs" (Colossians 3:16).</li>
        <li><strong>Let the words be the Word.</strong> "Let the word of Messiah dwell in you richly" (Colossians 3:16) — the same verse that commands the singing.</li>
        <li><strong>Sing with understanding.</strong> "I will sing with the spirit, and I will sing with the understanding also" (1 Corinthians 14:15).</li>
        <li><strong>Sing out of obedience.</strong> "If ye love me, keep my commandments" (John 14:15). The redeemed in Revelation sing "the song of Moses the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb" (Revelation 15:3).</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Is the Harm?</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul said the fight is "against principalities, against powers" (Ephesians 6:12). A principality does not need to stop the singing. It only needs to turn it around — from a song that rises out of the believer to Yahuah into a song that flows into the believer and centers on how he feels. Then the believer measures his worship by the feeling, and the feeling can be produced on demand, by a band, every week.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The enemy does not need to stop the song. He only needs to turn it around.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Babylon Goes Silent</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <blockquote class="cod-scripture">
            <p class="cod-scripture-text">And the voice of harpers, and musicians, and of pipers, and trumpeters, shall be heard no more at all in thee</p>
            <p class="cod-scripture-ref">— Revelation 18:22</p>
          </blockquote>
          <p class="cod-body">When the end-time system of Babylon falls, four kinds of musician are named as going silent: harpers, musicians, pipers, and trumpeters — the whole musical apparatus. They are listed in the same passage as her merchants and the blood of the saints. Music is one of the load-bearing pillars of Babylon's system, and when the system falls, its music falls with it.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The next verse says, "for by thy sorceries were all nations deceived" (Revelation 18:23). The word for sorceries is <em>pharmakeia</em>, the root of the English word pharmacy: drugs and the magical arts that alter the mind. Babylon's merchants, her musicians, and her sorceries are named together. A concert or a worship experience built to carry a crowd into an altered state does with sound what <em>pharmakeia</em> does with a potion.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">"Come out of her, my people, that ye be not partakers of her sins, and that ye receive not of her plagues" (Revelation 18:4). Come out — not reform her from within. A believer who brings Babylon's rhythms, production, and one-way flow with him, with Christian lyrics written over the top, has brought the piper along in his earbuds.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Verdict</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Church music is not a doctrine anyone defends. It is a practice, and every church has it, so no one checks it. Scripture gives music two directions: up, from a heart already changed, as praise to Yahuah; and across, between believers as equals, building each other up with the Word set to melody. The third direction — one-way, from a stage or a speaker into a listener — is the direction Scripture warns about, and it is the direction the modern Christian music industry is built on.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The record runs one way: from the congregation's psalms to the choir's Latin, from the hymnbook to the soloist, from the revival song that moved the crowd to the licensed, tracked, and streamed worship song of today. Every step moved the song further from the believer's own mouth and the Father's ear, and closer to the stage and the feeling. That is the direction a seducing spirit moves. In music, the enemy took the worship.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The question is not whether to sing. Miriam sang, David sang, Paul and Silas sang in prison, and the redeemed sing forever. The question is what is producing the song. If it rises out of a heart that loves the Father, keeps His commandments, trusts in the blood of His Son, and walks in His Spirit, it is a sweet offering. Revelation shows that song at the end: those who overcame the beast stand "having the harps of God. And they sing the song of Moses the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb" (Revelation 15:2-3).</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The piper is still calling. The Lamb is still being sung to. Every believer has to know which song is his.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-end"><span class="cod-end-mark">◆</span></div>
    </div>
  </article>
